  "final_text": "The dashboard and the rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana, so I haven't seen the panels render. The rule's status is still \"pending\", so it hasn't run yet.\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web access-log traffic. It has `timestamp`, `response` (a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `clientip`, `machine.os`, `host`, `request`, `url`, `referer` and `agent`. The `response` field only holds 200, 404 and 503 in the sample.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-f94e6e4b Web logs overview\" has five panels (default range is the last 7 days):\n- Total requests, as a single number\n- Requests over time, as a line chart\n- Requests by response code, as a pie chart\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart\n- Top source countries, as a table\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-f94e6e4b-web-logs-overview-61ef8b6fb266.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-f94e6e4b 5xx spike\" (id `ae28729f-2e9d-4288-a76b-b71332f3ba75`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and counts requests in the last 5 minutes whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\". It fires when that count is above 10. It has no actions attached, so a firing only shows up in the rule's status, with no notification.\n\nThe sample timestamps are fixed historical data, so the 5-minute window will probably count zero and the rule won't fire on this data.",
